NAME
    grep - search lines for a pattern

SYNOPSIS
    grep PATTERN [FILE...]
    grep -v PATTERN [FILE...]
    grep -f FIELD VALUE                 (JSONL mode)

DESCRIPTION
    Prints every line of input that contains PATTERN as a literal
    substring. PATTERN is NOT a regular expression in this OS yet —
    plain substring match only.

    With FILE arguments, reads each in turn. With no FILE arguments,
    reads stdin.

    -v inverts the match (print lines that do NOT contain PATTERN).

    -f FIELD VALUE (session 82) is the JSONL mode: input is parsed
    as JSONL records; output keeps every record where the FIELD's
    string value contains VALUE as a substring. Used inside `|>`
    pipelines.

EXAMPLES
    grep root /etc/passwd
    ps | grep init
    cat /etc/passwd | grep -v root
    ps |> grep -f name init             # structured pipeline

EXIT STATUS
    0   at least one matching line printed
    1   no lines matched
    2   I/O error

SEE ALSO
    cat(1), head(1), tail(1), sort(1), pluck(1), where(1)

NOTES
    No regex, no -E (extended), no -i (case-insensitive), no -r
    (recursive). Use find | grep for recursive search.
